/* KAT multiple-choice quiz, Units 3.1 to 3.8 (80 questions). Kept on the server so the answers are
   not in the page source. Every calculation was checked in code before this was written. */
export default {"id":"cat-mcq-1","title":"KAT multiple-choice quiz: Units 3.1 to 3.8","units":["3.1 What is business?","3.2 Managers, leadership and decision making","3.3 Decision making to improve marketing performance","3.4 Decision making to improve operational performance","3.5 Decision making to improve financial performance","3.6 Decision making to improve human resource performance","3.7 Analysing the strategic position of a business","3.8 Choosing strategic direction"],"items":[{"n":1,"u":0,"topic":"Market capitalisation (reverse)","ans":"B","stem":["Bexley Foods plc has a market capitalisation of £84 million. Its current share price is £3.50.","How many shares has Bexley Foods plc issued?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["2.4 million","24 million","240 million","294 million"],"work":["Market capitalisation = number of shares × share price","So number of shares = market capitalisation ÷ share price","£84m ÷ £3.50 = 24 million shares"],"right":"You have to work the formula backwards. Dividing the market capitalisation by the share price gives the number of shares.","wrong":{"A":"This is a place-value slip. £84m ÷ 3.5 is 24 million, not 2.4 million.","C":"Out by a factor of 10. Check by multiplying back: 240 million × £3.50 = £840m, not £84m.","D":"£84m × 3.5. You multiplied when you needed to divide."},"trap":"Reverse calculation. Write the formula out, then rearrange it before you touch the calculator."},{"n":2,"u":0,"topic":"Legal structure: Ltd and sole trader","ans":"C","stem":["Statement 1: ‘Shareholders in a private limited company can sell their shares to the general public on the stock market.’","Statement 2: ‘A sole trader’s business and its owner are not separate legal entities.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Statement 1 is false: only a public limited company can offer its shares to the public on the stock market. Shares in a private limited company can only be sold privately, usually with the agreement of the other shareholders. Statement 2 is true: a sole trader is unincorporated, so the owner and the business are the same in law. That is why the owner has unlimited liability.","wrong":{"A":"Statement 1 is false – private limited companies cannot sell shares to the public.","B":"This has both statements the wrong way round.","D":"Statement 2 is true. No incorporation means no separate legal identity."},"trap":"Students often think ‘limited company’ means shares can be traded on the stock market. That is only true of a plc."},{"n":3,"u":0,"topic":"Dividend per share","ans":"C","stem":["A company has 40 million ordinary shares. It pays out total dividends of £6 million for the year.","What is the dividend per share?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["0.15p","1.5p","15p","£1.50"],"work":["Dividend per share = total dividends ÷ number of shares","£6,000,000 ÷ 40,000,000 = £0.15 = 15p"],"right":"£0.15 per share is 15 pence.","wrong":{"A":"You have got £0.15 but labelled it in pence. £0.15 is 15p.","B":"This is a place-value slip, out by a factor of 10.","D":"Out by a factor of 10 in the other direction. Check by multiplying back: 40m × £1.50 = £60m, not £6m."},"trap":"Pounds and pence. Always multiply back to check you get the figure in the question."},{"n":4,"u":0,"topic":"Revenue from profit (reverse %)","ans":"D","stem":["A business made a profit of £45,000 last year. Its total costs were 70% of its revenue.","What was its revenue?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["£64,286","£76,500","£105,000","£150,000"],"work":["If total costs are 70% of revenue, profit is 100% − 70% = 30% of revenue","30% of revenue = £45,000","Revenue = £45,000 ÷ 0.30 = £150,000","Check: costs = 70% × £150,000 = £105,000; £150,000 − £105,000 = £45,000"],"right":"Profit is the 30% of revenue left after costs, so divide the profit by 0.3.","wrong":{"A":"£45,000 ÷ 0.7. This treats the profit as if it were the costs.","B":"£45,000 × 1.7. You cannot reverse a percentage by adding it on.","C":"This is the total costs, not the revenue. You stopped one step short."},"trap":"A reverse percentage. Work out what percentage the figure you have been given represents, then divide by it."},{"n":5,"u":0,"topic":"Share price and expectations","ans":"A","stem":["Which of the following is most likely to cause a fall in the share price of a public limited company?"],"table":null,"tpos":1,"opts":["The company announces that profits rose by 4%, when analysts had expected a rise of 10%.","The company announces a higher dividend than last year.","The Bank of England cuts interest rates.","There are rumours that a larger company is planning a takeover bid."],"work":null,"right":"Share prices move on expectations. If the market expected profits to rise by 10% and they only rose by 4%, the news is worse than expected, so investors sell and the price falls – even though profits went up.","wrong":{"B":"A higher dividend gives shareholders a better return, so demand for the shares usually rises.","C":"Lower interest rates make shares look better value than savings and should help profits, so share prices usually rise.","D":"A bidder usually has to offer more than the current price, so takeover rumours push the share price up."},"trap":"Rising profits do not always mean a rising share price. It depends on what investors were expecting."},{"n":6,"u":0,"topic":"Social enterprise","ans":"B","stem":["Statement 1: ‘A social enterprise has social or environmental aims as well as financial ones.’","Statement 2: ‘A social enterprise must be registered as a charity.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Statement 1 is true: a social enterprise trades to make a surplus but uses it mainly to achieve social or environmental aims. Statement 2 is false: many social enterprises are limited companies or community interest companies, not charities.","wrong":{"A":"Statement 2 is false. A social enterprise can take many legal forms.","C":"Statement 1 is true – that is what makes it a social enterprise.","D":"Statement 1 is true."},"trap":"A social enterprise can make a profit. What matters is what it does with it."},{"n":7,"u":0,"topic":"Mission v objectives","ans":"A","stem":["Which one of the following is most likely to be a corporate objective rather than a mission statement?"],"table":null,"tpos":1,"opts":["To increase our UK market share from 12% to 15% by the end of 2028.","To be the most trusted bank in Britain.","Making every journey better for our customers.","Our purpose is to serve the communities we work in."],"work":null,"right":"A corporate objective is a specific, measurable target with a time limit (SMART). A mission statement is a broad, qualitative statement of purpose. Only option A has a figure and a deadline.","wrong":{"B":"Broad and not measurable – this is a mission or vision statement.","C":"A slogan-style statement of purpose with no target or deadline.","D":"A statement of purpose, not a measurable target."},"trap":null},{"n":8,"u":0,"topic":"Minimum wage: costs and demand","ans":"B","stem":["The government makes a large increase in the national minimum wage.","For a supermarket that employs many low-paid staff and sells everyday groceries, what is the most likely effect? Assume the supermarket keeps its prices the same."],"table":{"head":["","Costs","Demand"],"rows":[["A","Increase","Decrease"],["B","Increase","Increase"],["C","Decrease","Increase"],["D","Decrease","Decrease"]]},"tpos":2,"opts":["Costs increase, demand decreases","Costs increase, demand increases","Costs decrease, demand increases","Costs decrease, demand decreases"],"work":null,"right":"The supermarket’s wage bill goes up, so costs rise. But low-paid workers across the economy now have more to spend, and they are a large part of a supermarket’s customer base, so demand is likely to rise as well.","wrong":{"A":"Costs rise, but there is no reason for demand to fall – incomes for low-paid customers have gone up.","C":"Costs cannot fall when the business has to pay its staff more.","D":"Neither part is right."},"trap":"Think about both sides of the business: what happens to its costs and what happens to its customers."},{"n":9,"u":0,"topic":"Interest rates and demand","ans":"D","stem":["A rise in interest rates is most likely to cause the biggest fall in demand for which of the following?"],"table":null,"tpos":1,"opts":["Toothpaste","Basic bread","Bus travel","New cars that customers usually buy using finance"],"work":null,"right":"Higher interest rates make borrowing more expensive. Products that customers usually buy on credit – cars, furniture, houses – see the biggest fall in demand because the monthly repayments go up.","wrong":{"A":"A cheap necessity. Demand hardly changes when interest rates change.","B":"A cheap necessity bought with cash.","C":"Low cost and often a necessity. If anything, some people switch to buses when car finance gets dearer."},"trap":null},{"n":10,"u":0,"topic":"Share dilution and control","ans":"D","stem":["An entrepreneur owns 600,000 of the 1,000,000 shares in her private limited company. To raise finance, the company issues 500,000 new shares to outside investors. She does not buy any of them.","What percentage of the company does she now own?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["60%","50%","45%","40%"],"work":["New total shares = 1,000,000 + 500,000 = 1,500,000","Her share = 600,000 ÷ 1,500,000 × 100 = 40%"],"right":"She still has 600,000 shares, but there are now 1.5 million shares in total. Her share has been diluted from 60% to 40%, so she has lost majority control.","wrong":{"A":"Her number of shares has not changed, but the total has, so her percentage has fallen.","B":"A guess. 50% would need 750,000 of 1.5 million.","C":"45% would mean owning 675,000 of the 1.5 million shares. It does not come from the figures in the question – check the new total number of shares."},"trap":"Raising finance through new shares can cost the original owners control. This is a good point to use in essays on sources of finance."},{"n":11,"u":1,"topic":"Decision tree: net gain","ans":"A","stem":["A business is deciding whether to launch a new product. The launch would cost £50,000.","There is a 0.6 chance of success, giving a pay-off of £150,000, and a 0.4 chance of failure, giving a pay-off of £20,000.","What is the net gain from launching the product?"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["£48,000","£98,000","£40,000","£35,000"],"work":["Expected value = (0.6 × £150,000) + (0.4 × £20,000)","= £90,000 + £8,000 = £98,000","Net gain = expected value − cost = £98,000 − £50,000 = £48,000"],"right":"Work out the expected value by weighting each pay-off by its probability, then take off the cost of the decision.","wrong":{"B":"This is the expected value. You forgot to take off the £50,000 cost.","C":"0.6 × £150,000 − £50,000. You left out the failure outcome.","D":"You averaged the two pay-offs without using the probabilities."},"trap":"Expected value and net gain are not the same thing. Net gain = expected value − cost."},{"n":12,"u":1,"topic":"Decision tree: probability (reverse)","ans":"B","stem":["A decision has two possible outcomes, with pay-offs of £100,000 and £40,000. The expected value of the decision is £64,000.","What is the probability of the £100,000 outcome?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["0.6","0.4","0.64","0.36"],"work":["Let p = probability of £100,000, so 1 − p = probability of £40,000","100,000p + 40,000(1 − p) = 64,000","60,000p = 24,000","p = 0.4","Check: (0.4 × 100,000) + (0.6 × 40,000) = 40,000 + 24,000 = 64,000"],"right":"Probabilities must add up to 1. Set up the expected value equation and solve for p. Always check by putting the answer back in.","wrong":{"A":"This is the probability of the £40,000 outcome – the right numbers the wrong way round.","C":"£64,000 ÷ £100,000. This ignores the other outcome.","D":"1 − 0.64. Based on the wrong starting figure."},"trap":"A reverse decision tree. Check your answer by working the expected value forwards."},{"n":13,"u":1,"topic":"Leadership styles","ans":"C","stem":["A manager makes all the decisions herself, but she explains the reasons to her staff and genuinely tries to act in their best interests.","Which leadership style is this?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["Autocratic","Democratic","Paternalistic","Laissez-faire"],"work":null,"right":"A paternalistic leader still makes the decisions, but does so with the welfare of staff in mind and explains why – like a parent.","wrong":{"A":"An autocratic leader also makes the decisions, but does not focus on staff welfare or explain the reasons. The care for staff is what makes this paternalistic.","B":"A democratic leader involves staff in making the decision. Here she makes them herself.","D":"A laissez-faire leader leaves staff to make most decisions themselves."},"trap":"Autocratic and paternalistic both keep decisions at the top. The difference is the concern for employees."},{"n":14,"u":1,"topic":"Tannenbaum and Schmidt","ans":"B","stem":["Statement 1: ‘On the Tannenbaum and Schmidt continuum, a move towards subordinate-centred leadership increases the area of freedom for subordinates.’","Statement 2: ‘Tannenbaum and Schmidt argued that there is one best leadership style which managers should use in all situations.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Statement 1 is true: the further along the continuum towards subordinate-centred leadership, the more freedom subordinates have and the less authority the manager uses. Statement 2 is false: their point is that the best style depends on the manager, the subordinates and the situation.","wrong":{"A":"Statement 2 is false. There is no single best style in their model.","C":"Statement 1 describes the continuum correctly.","D":"Statement 1 is true."},"trap":null},{"n":15,"u":1,"topic":"Stakeholder mapping","ans":"A","stem":["A business uses stakeholder mapping. A stakeholder group’s power increases sharply, but its interest in the business stays low.","How should the business change the way it treats this group?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["From ‘monitor’ to ‘keep satisfied’","From ‘keep informed’ to ‘manage closely’","From ‘monitor’ to ‘keep informed’","From ‘keep satisfied’ to ‘manage closely’"],"work":null,"right":"Low power, low interest = monitor (minimal effort). High power, low interest = keep satisfied. The group has moved up the power axis but not across the interest axis.","wrong":{"B":"‘Keep informed’ is low power, high interest. This group has low interest.","C":"‘Keep informed’ is for high interest. Power is what changed here, not interest.","D":"‘Manage closely’ needs high power and high interest. Interest is still low."},"trap":"Draw the grid in the margin – power on one axis, interest on the other – and plot where the group starts and finishes."},{"n":16,"u":1,"topic":"Shareholder v stakeholder concept","ans":"C","stem":["Statement 1: ‘The stakeholder concept means a business meets the needs of all of its stakeholder groups equally at all times.’","Statement 2: ‘Under the shareholder concept, managers make decisions mainly to maximise returns for the owners.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Statement 1 is false: the stakeholder concept means taking account of the interests of all stakeholders when making decisions, but their needs often conflict, so the business has to balance them. It cannot satisfy them all equally all of the time. Statement 2 is true.","wrong":{"A":"Statement 1 is false – the word ‘equally’ and ‘at all times’ make it wrong.","B":"The wrong way round.","D":"Statement 2 is a correct description of the shareholder concept."},"trap":"Watch for absolute words like ‘all’, ‘always’ and ‘equally’. They often make a statement false."},{"n":17,"u":1,"topic":"Opportunity cost","ans":"D","stem":["A business has £200,000 to invest. It has three choices:","• Project X, expected to earn £30,000 a year","• Project Y, expected to earn £26,000 a year","• leaving the money in the bank, earning £8,000 a year","Each option uses the full £200,000, so it can only choose one. It chooses Project X. What is the opportunity cost of this decision each year?"],"table":null,"tpos":5,"opts":["£4,000","£8,000","£34,000","£26,000"],"work":null,"right":"Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative given up. The next best alternative to Project X is Project Y, worth £26,000 a year.","wrong":{"A":"£30,000 − £26,000. This is how much better X is than Y, not what was given up.","B":"Leaving it in the bank was the worst option, not the next best.","C":"£26,000 + £8,000. You only give up one alternative – you could never have done all three."},"trap":"Opportunity cost is the next best alternative only, not every alternative added together."},{"n":18,"u":1,"topic":"Scientific v intuitive decisions","ans":"A","stem":["Statement 1: ‘Scientific decision-making is likely to be more useful when a lot of reliable data is available.’","Statement 2: ‘Intuitive decision-making is usually quicker than scientific decision-making.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Both are true. Scientific decision-making relies on data and analysis, so it works best when good data exists. Intuitive decisions are based on experience and gut feeling, so they are faster but riskier.","wrong":{"B":"Statement 2 is true – speed is the main advantage of intuition.","C":"Statement 1 is true.","D":"Both statements are true."},"trap":null},{"n":19,"u":1,"topic":"Leadership v management","ans":"A","stem":["Which one of the following is most closely linked to leadership rather than management?"],"table":null,"tpos":1,"opts":["Setting out a vision for the future and inspiring staff to work towards it","Setting budgets and checking variances each month","Organising staff rotas for the week","Checking that work meets the required standards"],"work":null,"right":"Leadership is about direction and people – setting a vision and getting others to follow it. Management is about planning, organising and controlling resources to get things done.","wrong":{"B":"Budgeting and monitoring are control tasks – management.","C":"Organising resources is a management task.","D":"Checking standards is control – management."},"trap":null},{"n":20,"u":1,"topic":"Decision trees: rules","ans":"C","stem":["Which one of the following statements about decision trees is correct?"],"table":null,"tpos":1,"opts":["The probabilities at a chance node can add up to more than 1.","A square on a decision tree shows a chance node.","The net gain of an option is its expected value minus its cost.","A manager should always choose the option with the highest possible pay-off."],"work":null,"right":"Net gain = expected value − cost of that option. Managers using the tree compare net gains.","wrong":{"A":"The probabilities at each chance node must add up to exactly 1, because one of the outcomes must happen.","B":"A square is a decision node. A circle is a chance node.","D":"The highest single pay-off may be very unlikely. The tree compares net gains, which take probability into account – and the manager may still weigh up risk."},"trap":null},{"n":21,"u":2,"topic":"PED and revenue","ans":"B","stem":["A product currently earns sales revenue of £400,000 a year. The business raises its price by 15%. The price elasticity of demand is −0.4.","What will the new sales revenue be?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["£376,000","£432,400","£436,000","£460,000"],"work":["% change in quantity = PED × % change in price = −0.4 × 15% = −6%","New revenue = £400,000 × 1.15 (price) × 0.94 (quantity)","= £432,400"],"right":"Demand is price inelastic, so quantity falls by less than the price rise and revenue goes up. Apply both changes to the revenue figure.","wrong":{"A":"£400,000 × 0.94. You applied the fall in quantity but forgot the higher price.","C":"£400,000 × 1.09. Adding 15% and −6% is only a rough estimate. The question asks for the new revenue, so multiply.","D":"£400,000 × 1.15. This assumes quantity does not change at all."},"trap":"Revenue = price × quantity, so both changes must be multiplied, not added."},{"n":22,"u":2,"topic":"Negative YED","ans":"D","stem":["A business sells 20,000 units of a product each year. The income elasticity of demand for the product is −0.6. Real incomes rise by 5%.","Assuming nothing else changes, how many units will it now sell?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["20,600","18,333","19,880","19,400"],"work":["% change in quantity demanded = YED × % change in income","= −0.6 × 5% = −3%","New sales = 20,000 × 0.97 = 19,400 units"],"right":"A negative YED means the product is an inferior good. When incomes rise, demand falls.","wrong":{"A":"You ignored the minus sign. Demand would rise only if YED were positive.","B":"5% ÷ 0.6. You divided instead of multiplying.","C":"−0.6% instead of −3%. You forgot to multiply by the 5% change."},"trap":"This is the topic the examiners said caused the most problems last summer. Negative YED: incomes up, demand down."},{"n":23,"u":2,"topic":"Market size (reverse %)","ans":"A","stem":["The UK market for plant-based yoghurt grew by 12.5% this year to £45 million.","What was the size of the market last year?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["£40.0 million","£39.4 million","£50.6 million","£32.5 million"],"work":["This year = last year × 1.125","Last year = £45m ÷ 1.125 = £40m","Check: £40m × 1.125 = £45m"],"right":"£45 million is 112.5% of last year’s figure, so divide by 1.125.","wrong":{"B":"£45m × 0.875 = £39.375m. Taking 12.5% off this year’s figure does not get you back to last year, because the 12.5% was worked out on last year’s smaller figure.","C":"£45m × 1.125. You added the growth again.","D":"£45m − 12.5. You took away 12.5 as if it were £12.5m."},"trap":"Last year’s Paper 2 asked a reverse percentage question and many students subtracted the percentage. Divide by 1.x."},{"n":24,"u":2,"topic":"Sampling methods","ans":"D","stem":["A business divides its target market into age groups. It then randomly selects people from each age group in proportion to the size of that group.","Which sampling method is this?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["Random","Quota","Focus group","Stratified"],"work":null,"right":"Stratified sampling splits the population into groups (strata) and then selects randomly from each group in proportion to its size.","wrong":{"A":"In random sampling everyone in the whole population has an equal chance of selection. There is no splitting into groups first.","B":"Quota sampling also uses groups, but the researcher picks people until each quota is filled – the selection is not random. The word ‘randomly’ is the clue here.","C":"A focus group is a qualitative primary research method, not a sampling method."},"trap":"Stratified and quota both use groups. Stratified selects randomly within groups; quota does not."},{"n":25,"u":2,"topic":"Correlation and extrapolation","ans":"C","stem":["Statement 1: ‘A strong positive correlation between advertising spending and sales proves that advertising causes sales to rise.’","Statement 2: ‘Extrapolation is likely to be less reliable when market conditions are changing quickly.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Statement 1 is false: correlation shows a relationship, not cause and effect. Sales might rise for other reasons, such as seasonal demand or rising incomes. Statement 2 is true: extrapolation assumes past trends continue, which is unlikely when the market is changing fast.","wrong":{"A":"Statement 1 is false – correlation does not prove causation.","B":"The wrong way round.","D":"Statement 2 is true."},"trap":"‘Proves’ is a strong word. Correlation never proves causation."},{"n":26,"u":2,"topic":"Boston Matrix and cash flow","ans":"B","stem":["Statement 1: ‘On the Boston Matrix, cash cows usually generate more cash than is needed to keep their market position.’","Statement 2: ‘On the Boston Matrix, stars usually generate a large positive net cash flow because they need little investment.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Statement 1 is true: cash cows have high market share in a low-growth market, so they need little investment and produce surplus cash. Statement 2 is false: stars bring in a lot of revenue but also need heavy investment to keep up with a fast-growing market, so their net cash flow is often small.","wrong":{"A":"Statement 2 is false – stars need a lot of investment.","C":"The wrong way round.","D":"Statement 1 is true."},"trap":"Do not confuse high sales with high net cash flow."},{"n":27,"u":2,"topic":"Cost-plus pricing (reverse)","ans":"D","stem":["A business uses cost-plus pricing. It sells a product for £30, which includes a 25% mark-up on cost.","What is the unit cost of the product?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["£22.50","£37.50","£7.50","£24.00"],"work":["Price = unit cost × 1.25","Unit cost = £30 ÷ 1.25 = £24","Check: £24 × 1.25 = £30"],"right":"The mark-up is 25% of cost, not of price, so the price is 125% of cost. Divide by 1.25.","wrong":{"A":"£30 × 0.75. Taking 25% off the price assumes the mark-up was on the price.","B":"£30 × 1.25. You added the mark-up again.","C":"£30 × 0.25. This is not the cost – and it is not even the mark-up (which is £6)."},"trap":"Reverse percentage again. A 25% mark-up on cost is not the same as 25% of the selling price."},{"n":28,"u":2,"topic":"Segmentation","ans":"C","stem":["A train company offers cheaper season tickets to commuters who travel every weekday. It also offers a railcard giving discounts to 16 to 25 year olds.","Which two methods of segmentation are being used?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["Geographic and income","Income and demographic","Behavioural and demographic","Geographic and behavioural"],"work":null,"right":"Grouping customers by how often they travel is behavioural segmentation (usage). Grouping by age is demographic segmentation.","wrong":{"A":"Neither offer is based on where people live or what they earn.","B":"Age is demographic, but nothing here is based on income.","D":"Frequency of travel is behavioural, but neither offer is based on location."},"trap":null},{"n":29,"u":2,"topic":"7Ps","ans":"A","stem":["A restaurant redesigns its dining area and trains its staff to be more knowledgeable and welcoming.","Which two elements of the marketing mix (7Ps) are these changes?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["Physical environment and people","Place and process","Product and people","Physical environment and process"],"work":null,"right":"The look and feel of the dining area is the physical environment. The staff and how they deal with customers is people.","wrong":{"B":"Place is about how customers get access to the product (distribution), not the décor.","C":"Training staff is people, but the dining area is the physical environment, not the product.","D":"The redesign is physical environment, but training staff to be welcoming is about people. Process is about the systems used to deliver the service."},"trap":null},{"n":30,"u":2,"topic":"Market share with growth","ans":"B","stem":["A market is worth £50 million. A business in the market has sales of £8 million.","Next year the market grows by 25% and the business’s sales grow by 10%.","What is the business’s new market share (to one decimal place)?"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["16.0%","14.1%","17.6%","12.8%"],"work":["New market size = £50m × 1.25 = £62.5m","New sales = £8m × 1.10 = £8.8m","Market share = £8.8m ÷ £62.5m × 100 = 14.08% ≈ 14.1%"],"right":"The business is growing more slowly than the market, so its market share falls from 16% to 14.1% even though its sales went up.","wrong":{"A":"This is the old market share (£8m ÷ £50m).","C":"£8.8m ÷ £50m. You updated the sales but not the market size.","D":"£8m ÷ £62.5m. You updated the market size but not the sales."},"trap":"Rising sales do not always mean rising market share. It depends on how fast the market is growing."},{"n":31,"u":3,"topic":"Capacity utilisation (reverse)","ans":"C","stem":["A factory produced 18,000 units last month. Its capacity utilisation was 72%.","What is the factory’s maximum capacity per month?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["12,960 units","30,960 units","25,000 units","7,000 units"],"work":["Capacity utilisation = output ÷ maximum capacity × 100","So maximum capacity = output ÷ 0.72","18,000 ÷ 0.72 = 25,000 units"],"right":"Rearrange the formula: maximum capacity = current output ÷ capacity utilisation.","wrong":{"A":"18,000 × 0.72. You multiplied instead of dividing – and the answer is smaller than the output, which cannot be right.","B":"18,000 × 1.72. You cannot reverse a percentage by adding it on.","D":"This is the spare capacity (25,000 − 18,000), not the maximum capacity."},"trap":"Sense check: maximum capacity must be bigger than actual output."},{"n":32,"u":3,"topic":"Labour productivity and cost per unit","ans":"C","stem":["The table shows data for a manufacturer.","Statement 1: ‘Labour productivity increased between 2024 and 2025.’","Statement 2: ‘Labour cost per unit stayed the same between 2024 and 2025.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":{"head":["","2024","2025"],"rows":[["Output (units)","48,000","55,200"],["Number of employees","40","46"],["Average pay per employee","£30,000","£30,000"]]},"tpos":1,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":["Labour productivity 2024 = 48,000 ÷ 40 = 1,200 units per employee","Labour productivity 2025 = 55,200 ÷ 46 = 1,200 units per employee – no change","Labour cost per unit 2024 = (40 × £30,000) ÷ 48,000 = £1,200,000 ÷ 48,000 = £25","Labour cost per unit 2025 = (46 × £30,000) ÷ 55,200 = £1,380,000 ÷ 55,200 = £25 – no change"],"right":"Output rose by 15%, but so did the number of employees, so output per worker stayed at 1,200. With pay per worker the same, labour cost per unit stays at £25.","wrong":{"A":"Statement 1 is false. More output does not mean higher productivity if the workforce grew at the same rate.","B":"Both parts are the wrong way round.","D":"Statement 2 is true – labour cost per unit is £25 in both years."},"trap":"Total output went up, which tempts you to say productivity went up. Always divide by the number of employees."},{"n":33,"u":3,"topic":"Unit costs","ans":"A","stem":["A business has fixed costs of £120,000 a year and variable costs of £5 per unit. Its output rises from 20,000 units to 30,000 units.","What happens to its unit cost (average cost per unit)?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["It falls from £11 to £9","It falls from £6 to £4","It stays at £11","It rises from £9 to £11"],"work":["Unit cost = total costs ÷ output","At 20,000: (£120,000 + 20,000 × £5) ÷ 20,000 = £220,000 ÷ 20,000 = £11","At 30,000: (£120,000 + 30,000 × £5) ÷ 30,000 = £270,000 ÷ 30,000 = £9"],"right":"Fixed costs are spread over more units, so the fixed cost per unit falls from £6 to £4. Variable cost per unit stays at £5.","wrong":{"B":"This is the fixed cost per unit only. You forgot to add the £5 variable cost.","C":"Unit cost does change, because the fixed costs are spread over more units.","D":"The right figures the wrong way round."},"trap":"This is why higher capacity utilisation lowers unit costs."},{"n":34,"u":3,"topic":"JIT v JIC","ans":"B","stem":["Statement 1: ‘A “Just in Case” approach to inventory reduces the risk of running out of stock if there are problems in the supply chain.’","Statement 2: ‘Just in Time production increases the amount of working capital tied up in inventory.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Statement 1 is true: Just in Case means holding extra buffer inventory, so the business can keep going if a delivery is late. Statement 2 is false: Just in Time keeps inventory to a minimum, so it reduces the working capital tied up in inventory.","wrong":{"A":"Statement 2 is false – JIT frees up working capital.","C":"The wrong way round.","D":"Statement 1 is true."},"trap":"JIT lowers inventory costs but raises the risk of running out. JIC is the reverse."},{"n":35,"u":3,"topic":"Reorder level","ans":"D","stem":["A shop uses the following inventory control system for one product:","• maximum inventory level: 900 units","• buffer inventory: 200 units","• usage: 100 units per day","• lead time: 3 days","At what level of inventory should it place a new order (the reorder level)?"],"table":null,"tpos":6,"opts":["700 units","300 units","1,100 units","500 units"],"work":["Reorder level = buffer inventory + (usage per day × lead time)","= 200 + (100 × 3) = 500 units","Check: ordering at 500, the shop uses 300 units while waiting, so the delivery arrives just as inventory reaches the buffer of 200."],"right":"The order must be placed early enough that the delivery arrives as inventory falls to the buffer level.","wrong":{"A":"This is the reorder quantity (900 − 200), not the reorder level.","B":"This is the usage during the lead time. You forgot to add the buffer inventory.","C":"900 + 200. This is above the maximum level, which makes no sense."},"trap":"Reorder level (when to order) and reorder quantity (how much to order) are different things."},{"n":36,"u":3,"topic":"QA v QC","ans":"D","stem":["Statement 1: ‘Quality control aims to prevent faults from happening in the first place.’","Statement 2: ‘Quality assurance relies on inspecting finished products at the end of the production line.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Both statements are false – they have swapped the two systems. Quality control inspects products, usually at the end, to find faults. Quality assurance tries to prevent faults by building quality into every stage of the process.","wrong":{"A":"Both statements describe the other system.","B":"Statement 1 describes quality assurance, not quality control.","C":"Statement 2 describes quality control, not quality assurance."},"trap":"The examiners named QA and QC as a pair that students mix up. Control = detect. Assurance = prevent."},{"n":37,"u":3,"topic":"Capacity utilisation: actions","ans":"A","stem":["Assuming total annual demand stays the same, which one of the following would increase a manufacturer’s capacity utilisation?"],"table":null,"tpos":1,"opts":["Closing one of its three factories while keeping total output the same","Outsourcing part of its production to another firm","Buying extra machinery to increase its maximum output","Demand for its products becoming more seasonal"],"work":null,"right":"Closing a factory reduces maximum capacity. If output stays the same, output ÷ capacity rises. This is rationalisation.","wrong":{"B":"Outsourcing moves output away from its own factories, so its own output falls and utilisation falls.","C":"Extra machinery increases capacity. With the same output, utilisation falls.","D":"Seasonal demand means factories are underused for part of the year, so average utilisation falls."},"trap":null},{"n":38,"u":3,"topic":"Automation","ans":"C","stem":["Statement 1: ‘Increasing automation is likely to make a business more labour intensive.’","Statement 2: ‘Automation may only reduce unit costs if output is high enough to spread the cost of the machinery.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Statement 1 is false: automation replaces labour with machinery, so the business becomes more capital intensive. Statement 2 is true: automation raises fixed costs, so it only lowers unit costs if output is high enough.","wrong":{"A":"Statement 1 is false.","B":"The wrong way round.","D":"Statement 2 is true."},"trap":null},{"n":39,"u":3,"topic":"Choosing suppliers","ans":"B","stem":["A business switches to a new supplier which is cheaper but less reliable at delivering on time.","What is the most likely effect?"],"table":{"head":["","Unit costs of materials","Level of buffer inventory needed"],"rows":[["A","Fall","Fall"],["B","Fall","Rise"],["C","Rise","Rise"],["D","Rise","Fall"]]},"tpos":2,"opts":["Fall, fall","Fall, rise","Rise, rise","Rise, fall"],"work":null,"right":"A cheaper supplier lowers the unit cost of materials. But late deliveries mean the business needs more buffer inventory to avoid running out, which adds holding costs.","wrong":{"A":"An unreliable supplier means you need more buffer inventory, not less.","C":"The supplier is cheaper, so unit costs of materials fall.","D":"Both parts are wrong."},"trap":"Price is not the only factor when choosing a supplier. Reliability affects inventory costs too."},{"n":40,"u":3,"topic":"Capacity utilisation: two changes","ans":"A","stem":["A factory has a maximum capacity of 40,000 units and operates at 85% capacity utilisation.","It buys new machinery, which increases maximum capacity by 25%. Output then increases by 10%.","What is its new capacity utilisation?"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["74.8%","93.5%","85.0%","68.0%"],"work":["Current output = 40,000 × 0.85 = 34,000 units","New output = 34,000 × 1.10 = 37,400 units","New capacity = 40,000 × 1.25 = 50,000 units","New capacity utilisation = 37,400 ÷ 50,000 × 100 = 74.8%"],"right":"Capacity has grown faster than output, so utilisation falls.","wrong":{"B":"37,400 ÷ 40,000. You used the old capacity.","C":"Utilisation does change because capacity and output grow at different rates.","D":"34,000 ÷ 50,000. You used the old output."},"trap":"When two things change, change both before you divide."},{"n":41,"u":4,"topic":"Break-even: price (reverse)","ans":"C","stem":["A product breaks even at 2,500 units. Its fixed costs are £40,000 and its variable cost is £9 per unit.","What is its selling price?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["£16","£7","£25","£34"],"work":["Break-even = fixed costs ÷ contribution per unit","So contribution per unit = £40,000 ÷ 2,500 = £16","Selling price = contribution + variable cost = £16 + £9 = £25","Check: £40,000 ÷ (£25 − £9) = 2,500"],"right":"Rearrange to find contribution per unit, then add the variable cost back on.","wrong":{"A":"This is the contribution per unit. You stopped one step short.","B":"£16 − £9. Price = contribution + variable cost, not minus.","D":"You added the variable cost twice."},"trap":"Reverse break-even. Contribution is not the same as price."},{"n":42,"u":4,"topic":"Margin of safety","ans":"A","stem":["A product sells for £50 and has a variable cost of £30 per unit. Fixed costs are £180,000. At its current output it makes a profit of £60,000.","What is its margin of safety?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["3,000 units","£60,000","12,000 units","9,000 units"],"work":["Contribution per unit = £50 − £30 = £20","Break-even = £180,000 ÷ £20 = 9,000 units","Total contribution = fixed costs + profit = £180,000 + £60,000 = £240,000","Current output = £240,000 ÷ £20 = 12,000 units","Margin of safety = 12,000 − 9,000 = 3,000 units","(Quicker: profit ÷ contribution per unit = £60,000 ÷ £20 = 3,000 units)"],"right":"Margin of safety = current output − break-even output, measured in units.","wrong":{"B":"This is the profit, not the margin of safety. Margin of safety is measured in units.","C":"This is the current output.","D":"This is the break-even output."},"trap":"In June 2026 half the students lost marks on margin of safety, many by giving it in pounds or confusing it with profit."},{"n":43,"u":4,"topic":"Operating expenses from margin (reverse)","ans":"B","stem":["A business has revenue of £800,000 and gross profit of £300,000. Its operating profit margin is 12%.","What were its operating expenses?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["£96,000","£204,000","£396,000","£704,000"],"work":["Operating profit = 12% × £800,000 = £96,000","Operating profit = gross profit − operating expenses","So operating expenses = £300,000 − £96,000 = £204,000"],"right":"Use the margin to find operating profit, then work backwards from gross profit.","wrong":{"A":"This is the operating profit, not the operating expenses.","C":"£300,000 + £96,000. Operating expenses are taken away from gross profit, so you subtract.","D":"£800,000 − £96,000. This mixes up revenue with gross profit."},"trap":"A multi-step question like last year’s Paper 2. Write the income statement out in order: revenue, cost of sales, gross profit, operating expenses, operating profit."},{"n":44,"u":4,"topic":"Income statement","ans":"C","stem":["Statement 1: ‘An increase in the salaries of office staff will reduce a business’s gross profit.’","Statement 2: ‘A fall in the interest a business pays on its loans will increase its profit for the year but not its operating profit.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Statement 1 is false: office salaries are operating expenses, so they reduce operating profit but not gross profit. Gross profit is only affected by revenue and cost of sales. Statement 2 is true: interest comes after operating profit in the income statement.","wrong":{"A":"Statement 1 is false.","B":"The wrong way round.","D":"Statement 2 is true."},"trap":"Know exactly where each cost sits in the income statement."},{"n":45,"u":4,"topic":"Budget variance","ans":"D","stem":["A business budgeted for revenue of £250,000 and costs of £190,000.","Actual revenue was 8% below budget. Actual costs were 5% below budget.","What was the profit variance?"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["£29,500 adverse","£10,500 favourable","£20,000 adverse","£10,500 adverse"],"work":["Budgeted profit = £250,000 − £190,000 = £60,000","Actual revenue = £250,000 × 0.92 = £230,000","Actual costs = £190,000 × 0.95 = £180,500","Actual profit = £230,000 − £180,500 = £49,500","Variance = £60,000 − £49,500 = £10,500 adverse (actual profit is lower than budgeted)"],"right":"Actual profit is below the budgeted profit, so the variance is adverse.","wrong":{"A":"You treated the £9,500 saving on costs as adverse. Lower costs are a favourable variance.","B":"The right figure, but profit is lower than planned, so it is adverse.","C":"This is only the revenue variance. You ignored the cost saving."},"trap":"Lower costs = favourable. Lower revenue or lower profit = adverse."},{"n":46,"u":4,"topic":"Cash flow: opening balance (reverse)","ans":"B","stem":["A business’s cash flow forecast shows a closing balance of £4,000 for March. In March, cash inflows were £38,000 and cash outflows were £45,000.","What was the opening balance for March?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["−£3,000","£11,000","£7,000","−£11,000"],"work":["Net cash flow = £38,000 − £45,000 = −£7,000","Closing balance = opening balance + net cash flow","£4,000 = opening + (−£7,000)","Opening balance = £4,000 + £7,000 = £11,000"],"right":"The business spent £7,000 more than it received during March and still finished with £4,000, so it must have started with £11,000.","wrong":{"A":"£4,000 − £7,000. This works the month forwards instead of backwards.","C":"This is the size of the net outflow, not the opening balance.","D":"The right number with the wrong sign."},"trap":"Reverse cash flow. Write the three lines of the forecast out and fill in the gap."},{"n":47,"u":4,"topic":"Debt factoring: cash v profit","ans":"B","stem":["Statement 1: ‘Debt factoring improves a business’s cash flow because it receives most of the value of its invoices straight away.’","Statement 2: ‘Debt factoring increases a business’s profit because it receives the money from its customers sooner.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Statement 1 is true: the factor pays the business most of the value of its receivables immediately. Statement 2 is false: the factor charges a fee, so the business receives less than the full value of its sales. Debt factoring improves cash flow but reduces profit.","wrong":{"A":"Statement 2 is false – factoring costs money, so profit falls.","C":"Statement 1 is true.","D":"Statement 1 is true."},"trap":"Cash flow and profit are not the same. Debt factoring was a big knowledge gap on last year’s Paper 1."},{"n":48,"u":4,"topic":"Sources of finance","ans":"D","stem":["Which one of the following is an external source of finance that does not have to be repaid?"],"table":null,"tpos":1,"opts":["Retained profit","Bank loan","Overdraft","Issuing new shares"],"work":null,"right":"Share capital comes from outside the business and is never repaid – shareholders get a return through dividends and a rising share price instead.","wrong":{"A":"Retained profit does not need to be repaid, but it is an internal source.","B":"External, but must be repaid with interest.","C":"External, but must be repaid with interest."},"trap":null},{"n":49,"u":4,"topic":"Break-even: contribution","ans":"A","stem":["A business increases the selling price of its product by £3. At the same time its variable cost per unit also increases by £3. Fixed costs do not change.","What happens to its break-even output?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["It stays the same","It rises","It falls","It cannot be worked out without more information"],"work":null,"right":"Contribution per unit = price − variable cost. Both rise by £3, so contribution per unit is unchanged. With fixed costs the same, break-even output does not change.","wrong":{"B":"Break-even only rises if contribution per unit falls or fixed costs rise.","C":"The higher price is cancelled out by the higher variable cost.","D":"You have all you need: break-even = fixed costs ÷ contribution per unit, and neither has changed."},"trap":"Do not look at one change in isolation. Ask what happens to contribution per unit."},{"n":50,"u":4,"topic":"Profit v cash flow methods","ans":"C","stem":["Statement 1: ‘Cutting the price of a product with price inelastic demand will increase the business’s profit.’","Statement 2: ‘Sale and leaseback of property improves a business’s cash flow in the short term.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Statement 1 is false: if demand is price inelastic, a price cut raises sales by a smaller proportion, so revenue falls – and with more units to make, costs rise – so profit falls. Statement 2 is true: selling the property brings in a large sum of cash now, although rent must then be paid.","wrong":{"A":"Statement 1 is false.","B":"The wrong way round.","D":"Statement 2 is true."},"trap":null},{"n":51,"u":5,"topic":"Labour turnover","ans":"C","stem":["A business employed 180 staff at the start of the year and 220 at the end. During the year 30 staff left.","What was its labour turnover?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["13.6%","16.7%","15.0%","7.5%"],"work":["Average number of staff = (180 + 220) ÷ 2 = 200","Labour turnover = number leaving ÷ average number employed × 100","= 30 ÷ 200 × 100 = 15%"],"right":"Labour turnover uses the average number employed over the period.","wrong":{"A":"30 ÷ 220. You used the closing number of staff.","B":"30 ÷ 180. You used the opening number of staff.","D":"30 ÷ 400. You added the two figures but did not halve them."},"trap":"Use the average number of employees."},{"n":52,"u":5,"topic":"Labour turnover (reverse)","ans":"A","stem":["A business had a labour turnover rate of 12% last year. On average it employed 350 staff.","How many staff left during the year?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["42","29","308","392"],"work":["Labour turnover = leavers ÷ average staff × 100","12 = leavers ÷ 350 × 100","Leavers = 350 × 0.12 = 42"],"right":"Rearrange the formula: leavers = labour turnover × average number of staff.","wrong":{"B":"350 ÷ 12. You divided instead of multiplying.","C":"350 × 0.88. This is the number who did not leave.","D":"350 × 1.12. This has no meaning here."},"trap":null},{"n":53,"u":5,"topic":"Labour cost per unit (reverse)","ans":"C","stem":["A business’s labour cost per unit is £4. Each employee is paid £28,000 a year.","What is its labour productivity (output per employee per year)?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["112,000 units","700 units","7,000 units","70,000 units"],"work":["Labour cost per unit = labour costs ÷ output","For one employee: £4 = £28,000 ÷ output per employee","Output per employee = £28,000 ÷ £4 = 7,000 units"],"right":"Labour cost per unit = pay per worker ÷ output per worker. Rearranging gives output per worker = pay ÷ labour cost per unit.","wrong":{"A":"£28,000 × 4. You multiplied instead of dividing.","B":"Out by a factor of 10.","D":"Out by a factor of 10."},"trap":"Labour productivity and labour cost per unit move in opposite directions: if productivity rises and pay stays the same, cost per unit falls."},{"n":54,"u":5,"topic":"Employee costs % of turnover","ans":"B","stem":["The table shows data for a business.","What are employee costs as a percentage of turnover this year?"],"table":{"head":["","Last year","This year"],"rows":[["Revenue","£2.4m","Up 25%"],["Employee costs","£600,000","Up 10%"]]},"tpos":1,"opts":["27.5%","22.0%","25.0%","20.0%"],"work":["New revenue = £2.4m × 1.25 = £3.0m","New employee costs = £600,000 × 1.10 = £660,000","Employee costs as % of turnover = £660,000 ÷ £3,000,000 × 100 = 22%"],"right":"Revenue has grown faster than employee costs, so the percentage falls from 25% to 22%.","wrong":{"A":"£660,000 ÷ £2.4m. You updated employee costs but not revenue.","C":"This is last year’s figure.","D":"£600,000 ÷ £3.0m. You updated revenue but not employee costs."},"trap":"Update both figures before dividing. ‘Turnover’ here means revenue, not labour turnover."},{"n":55,"u":5,"topic":"Herzberg","ans":"C","stem":["Statement 1: ‘Herzberg argued that improving working conditions will motivate employees to work harder.’","Statement 2: ‘Herzberg argued that job enrichment can motivate employees because it gives them more responsibility.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Statement 1 is false: working conditions are a hygiene factor. Improving them removes dissatisfaction but does not motivate. Statement 2 is true: responsibility is a motivator, and job enrichment adds more of it to the job.","wrong":{"A":"Statement 1 is false – working conditions are a hygiene factor.","B":"The wrong way round.","D":"Statement 2 is true."},"trap":"Pay and working conditions are hygiene factors. Last summer’s examiners said many students wrongly claimed pay motivates under Herzberg."},{"n":56,"u":5,"topic":"Maslow","ans":"B","stem":["A business moves staff who were on zero-hours contracts onto permanent contracts with a company pension.","Which level of Maslow’s hierarchy of needs is this mainly meeting?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["Physiological","Safety","Love and belonging (social)","Esteem"],"work":null,"right":"A permanent contract and a pension give job security and financial security, which are safety needs.","wrong":{"A":"Physiological needs are basics such as food, water and enough pay to live on. The change here is about security.","C":"Social needs are about friendship and being part of a team.","D":"Esteem needs are about recognition, status and respect."},"trap":null},{"n":57,"u":5,"topic":"Taylor","ans":"D","stem":["Which one of the following is most closely linked to Taylor’s scientific management?"],"table":null,"tpos":1,"opts":["Team working","Job enrichment","Empowering workers to decide how to do their jobs","Breaking jobs into small tasks, setting the best way to do each one and paying piece rate"],"work":null,"right":"Taylor believed there was one best way to do each task, found by time-and-motion study. Workers were trained to do it that way and paid piece rate, because he saw money as the main motivator.","wrong":{"A":"Team working comes from the human relations approach, not Taylor.","B":"Job enrichment comes from Herzberg.","C":"Taylor wanted managers to decide how work is done, not workers."},"trap":null},{"n":58,"u":5,"topic":"Span of control and levels","ans":"A","stem":["A business has a managing director at the top. Every manager in the business, including the managing director, has a span of control of 4. There are 4 levels of hierarchy, including the managing director.","How many people work in the business in total?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["85","64","256","21"],"work":["Level 1: 1 (the managing director)","Level 2: 1 × 4 = 4","Level 3: 4 × 4 = 16","Level 4: 16 × 4 = 64","Total = 1 + 4 + 16 + 64 = 85"],"right":"Each level has four times as many people as the one above. Add all four levels together.","wrong":{"B":"This is only the bottom level.","C":"This would be a fifth level (64 × 4).","D":"1 + 4 + 16. You missed the fourth level."},"trap":null},{"n":59,"u":5,"topic":"Delayering and decentralisation","ans":"A","stem":["Statement 1: ‘Delayering is likely to increase the average span of control.’","Statement 2: ‘Decentralisation means passing authority for decisions down to managers in branches or divisions.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Both are true. Removing a layer of management means the remaining managers have more people reporting to them. Decentralisation moves decision-making authority away from head office.","wrong":{"B":"Statement 2 is a correct definition of decentralisation.","C":"Statement 1 is true.","D":"Both statements are true."},"trap":"Delegation, delayering and decentralisation are three different things – the examiners keep seeing them confused."},{"n":60,"u":5,"topic":"Organisational structures","ans":"D","stem":["In a business, each employee reports to a manager in their own department and also to a project manager.","What type of organisational structure is this?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["Functional","Product-based","Regional","Matrix"],"work":null,"right":"In a matrix structure, staff have two lines of reporting: to a functional (department) manager and to a project or product manager.","wrong":{"A":"A functional structure groups staff by department only, with one line of reporting.","B":"A product-based structure groups staff by product division.","C":"A regional structure groups staff by geographical area."},"trap":null},{"n":61,"u":6,"topic":"ARR","ans":"B","stem":["A business is considering a project costing £120,000. The expected net returns are shown below.","What is the average rate of return (ARR) for the project?"],"table":{"head":["Year","Net return (£)"],"rows":[["1","40,000"],["2","45,000"],["3","50,000"],["4","25,000"]]},"tpos":1,"opts":["33.3%","8.3%","25.0%","6.3%"],"work":["Total net returns = 40,000 + 45,000 + 50,000 + 25,000 = £160,000","Total profit = £160,000 − £120,000 = £40,000","Average annual profit = £40,000 ÷ 4 = £10,000","ARR = £10,000 ÷ £120,000 × 100 = 8.3%"],"right":"Take off the initial cost first, then find the average annual profit and divide by the initial investment.","wrong":{"A":"£160,000 ÷ 4 ÷ £120,000. You forgot to take off the initial cost – the most common ARR mistake.","C":"£40,000 ÷ £160,000. You divided total profit by total returns.","D":"£10,000 ÷ £160,000. You divided by the total returns instead of the initial investment."},"trap":"Only about 20% of students got the ARR question fully right in June 2026. The main error was not subtracting the initial cost."},{"n":62,"u":6,"topic":"Payback","ans":"C","stem":["A project costs £90,000. It is expected to bring in net cash flows of £30,000 in Year 1, £40,000 in Year 2 and £48,000 in Year 3. Assume cash flows are spread evenly through each year.","What is the payback period?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["2 years 4 months","2 years 6 months","2 years 5 months","3 years"],"work":["After Year 1: £30,000 recovered","After Year 2: £30,000 + £40,000 = £70,000 recovered","Still needed: £90,000 − £70,000 = £20,000","Months into Year 3 = £20,000 ÷ £48,000 × 12 = 5 months","Payback = 2 years 5 months"],"right":"Find how much is still needed at the end of Year 2 and divide by the Year 3 cash flow, then multiply by 12.","wrong":{"A":"20,000 ÷ 48,000 = 0.42 of a year, which is 5 months, not 4. Convert to months by multiplying by 12.","B":"You divided by the Year 2 cash flow (£40,000) instead of Year 3.","D":"The investment is paid back during Year 3, not at the end of it."},"trap":"0.42 years is not 4 months. Multiply by 12."},{"n":63,"u":6,"topic":"NPV","ans":"D","stem":["A project costs £50,000 now and will bring in net returns of £30,000 at the end of each of the next two years.","What is the net present value (NPV) of the project?"],"table":{"head":["Year","Net return (£)","Discount factor"],"rows":[["1","30,000","0.926"],["2","30,000","0.857"]]},"tpos":1,"opts":["£53,490","£10,000","−£3,490","£3,490"],"work":["Present value Year 1 = £30,000 × 0.926 = £27,780","Present value Year 2 = £30,000 × 0.857 = £25,710","Total present value = £53,490","NPV = £53,490 − £50,000 = £3,490"],"right":"Discount each year’s return, add them up, then take off the initial cost. A positive NPV means the project earns more than the discount rate.","wrong":{"A":"This is the total present value. You forgot to take off the initial cost.","B":"£60,000 − £50,000. You did not discount the returns.","C":"The right number with the wrong sign. The present value is bigger than the cost, so NPV is positive."},"trap":"Take off the initial cost. It is the same mistake as in ARR."},{"n":64,"u":6,"topic":"ROCE (reverse)","ans":"A","stem":["A company has total equity of £3.2 million and non-current liabilities of £1.8 million. Its return on capital employed (ROCE) is 14%.","What is its operating profit?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["£700,000","£448,000","£252,000","£35.7 million"],"work":["Capital employed = total equity + non-current liabilities = £3.2m + £1.8m = £5.0m","ROCE = operating profit ÷ capital employed × 100","Operating profit = £5.0m × 0.14 = £700,000"],"right":"Find capital employed first, then rearrange the ROCE formula.","wrong":{"B":"£3.2m × 0.14. You used total equity only, not capital employed.","C":"£1.8m × 0.14. You used non-current liabilities only.","D":"£5.0m ÷ 0.14. You divided instead of multiplying."},"trap":"Working back from ROCE came up on Paper 1 in 2025. Capital employed includes non-current liabilities."},{"n":65,"u":6,"topic":"Gearing after a loan","ans":"C","stem":["A company has total equity of £6 million and non-current liabilities of £2 million. It takes out a new £2 million long-term loan to buy machinery.","What happens to its gearing?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["It rises from 33.3% to 66.7%","It rises from 25% to 50%","It rises from 25% to 40%","It rises from 25% to 33.3%"],"work":["Gearing = non-current liabilities ÷ (total equity + non-current liabilities) × 100","Before: £2m ÷ (£6m + £2m) = 25%","After: £4m ÷ (£6m + £4m) = £4m ÷ £10m = 40%"],"right":"The new loan increases non-current liabilities and capital employed by the same amount, so both the top and bottom of the ratio change.","wrong":{"A":"These divide non-current liabilities by total equity only. That is not the AQA gearing formula.","B":"£4m ÷ £8m. You updated the loans but not capital employed.","D":"£4m ÷ £12m. You added the new loan to capital employed twice. Capital employed is £6m + £4m = £10m."},"trap":"When loans change, capital employed changes too."},{"n":66,"u":6,"topic":"Receivables and payables days","ans":"B","stem":["The data below is taken from a company’s accounts.","Statement 1: ‘Receivables days are 30 days.’","Statement 2: ‘Payables days are 20 days.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":{"head":["","£"],"rows":[["Revenue","1,095,000"],["Cost of sales","730,000"],["Receivables","90,000"],["Payables","60,000"]]},"tpos":1,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":["Receivables days = receivables ÷ revenue × 365 = 90,000 ÷ 1,095,000 × 365 = 30 days","Payables days = payables ÷ cost of sales × 365 = 60,000 ÷ 730,000 × 365 = 30 days"],"right":"Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false: payables days are 30 days, not 20. Payables days are calculated using cost of sales, not revenue.","wrong":{"A":"20 days comes from dividing payables by revenue. The formula uses cost of sales.","C":"Statement 1 is correct.","D":"Statement 1 is correct."},"trap":"Receivables use revenue. Payables use cost of sales."},{"n":67,"u":6,"topic":"Current ratio after a change","ans":"D","stem":["A business has current assets of £400,000 and current liabilities of £250,000. It uses £50,000 of cash to pay off part of its overdraft.","What is its new current ratio?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["1.40","1.60","2.00","1.75"],"work":["Before: £400,000 ÷ £250,000 = 1.6","Cash falls by £50,000: current assets = £350,000","Overdraft falls by £50,000: current liabilities = £200,000","After: £350,000 ÷ £200,000 = 1.75"],"right":"Paying off the overdraft with cash reduces both current assets and current liabilities by the same amount. Because the ratio was above 1, it rises.","wrong":{"A":"£350,000 ÷ £250,000. You reduced the cash but forgot the overdraft is a current liability.","B":"The ratio does change when both figures fall by the same amount.","C":"£400,000 ÷ £200,000. You reduced the overdraft but forgot the cash used to pay it."},"trap":"Each transaction affects two figures. Change both."},{"n":68,"u":6,"topic":"Exchange rates","ans":"B","stem":["A UK firm exports a product to the eurozone and keeps its price at £200. The exchange rate changes from £1 = €1.10 to £1 = €1.25.","What happens to the price paid by eurozone customers?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["It falls from €181.82 to €160.00","It rises from €220 to €250","It falls from €250 to €220","It stays the same"],"work":["Before: £200 × 1.10 = €220","After: £200 × 1.25 = €250"],"right":"The pound has strengthened, so each pound buys more euros. A product priced in pounds now costs more in euros, which makes UK exports less competitive.","wrong":{"A":"You divided by the exchange rate. To turn pounds into euros, multiply.","C":"The right figures the wrong way round.","D":"The sterling price is the same, but the euro price changes with the exchange rate."},"trap":"SPICED: Strong Pound, Imports Cheaper, Exports Dearer."},{"n":69,"u":6,"topic":"Porter: supplier power","ans":"A","stem":["Using Porter’s five forces, which one of the following is most likely to reduce the bargaining power of a business’s suppliers?"],"table":null,"tpos":1,"opts":["The business finds several new suppliers that offer a standard, identical product","Two of the main suppliers merge","The cost of switching to a different supplier rises","The business’s orders become a much smaller share of each supplier’s total sales"],"work":null,"right":"If there are many suppliers offering the same product, the business can easily switch, so suppliers cannot push prices up.","wrong":{"B":"Fewer suppliers means more supplier power.","C":"Higher switching costs lock the business in, which increases supplier power.","D":"If the business is a less important customer, suppliers depend on it less, so their power increases."},"trap":null},{"n":70,"u":6,"topic":"SWOT and Elkington","ans":"C","stem":["Statement 1: ‘In a SWOT analysis, a rise in interest rates is an internal weakness.’","Statement 2: ‘Elkington’s triple bottom line measures performance in terms of profit, people and planet.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Statement 1 is false: interest rates are part of the external environment, so a rise would be a threat, not a weakness. Strengths and weaknesses are internal. Statement 2 is true.","wrong":{"A":"Statement 1 is false.","B":"The wrong way round.","D":"Statement 2 is a correct description of Elkington’s triple bottom line."},"trap":"Strengths and weaknesses are internal. Opportunities and threats are external."},{"n":71,"u":7,"topic":"Ansoff: market development","ans":"B","stem":["A UK bakery chain sells sandwiches to the public in its high street shops. It starts selling the same sandwiches to offices through a new corporate delivery service.","According to Ansoff’s matrix, this is an example of:"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["market penetration.","market development.","product development.","diversification."],"work":null,"right":"The product is the same but the customers are new (businesses instead of individual shoppers). A new market segment counts as a new market, so this is market development.","wrong":{"A":"Market penetration means selling more of the existing product to the existing market. Offices are a new market.","C":"The sandwiches are not a new product.","D":"Diversification needs a new product and a new market. The product is not new."},"trap":"A new market does not have to be a new country. A new segment counts."},{"n":72,"u":7,"topic":"Ansoff: risk and definitions","ans":"D","stem":["Statement 1: ‘According to Ansoff’s matrix, diversification is usually the lowest-risk strategy because the business spreads its risk across different markets.’","Statement 2: ‘According to Ansoff’s matrix, product development means selling existing products in new markets.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Both are false. Diversification is usually the highest-risk strategy, because the business has no experience of either the product or the market. Product development means new products in existing markets – existing products in new markets is market development.","wrong":{"A":"Both statements are false.","B":"Diversification is the most risky option on Ansoff’s matrix.","C":"Statement 2 describes market development."},"trap":"Spreading risk is a reason firms diversify, but Ansoff ranks diversification as the most risky strategy."},{"n":73,"u":7,"topic":"Porter: differentiation focus","ans":"D","stem":["A business targets a small group of very wealthy customers with a handmade, highly customised luxury product.","Which of Porter’s generic strategies is this?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["Cost leadership","Differentiation","Cost focus","Differentiation focus"],"work":null,"right":"A narrow target market (focus) plus a unique, premium product (differentiation) = differentiation focus.","wrong":{"A":"Cost leadership targets a broad market with the lowest costs.","B":"Differentiation targets a broad market. This business targets a small group.","C":"Cost focus targets a narrow market by being the low-cost option. This is a luxury product."},"trap":null},{"n":74,"u":7,"topic":"Porter: cost leadership","ans":"B","stem":["Statement 1: ‘Porter warned that a business trying to be both low cost and differentiated risks being “stuck in the middle”.’","Statement 2: ‘A cost leadership strategy means charging the lowest prices in the market.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false: cost leadership means having the lowest costs in the industry. The business may charge low prices, or charge an average price and earn a higher margin than its rivals.","wrong":{"A":"Statement 2 is false – cost leadership is about costs, not necessarily prices.","C":"Statement 1 is true.","D":"Statement 1 is true."},"trap":"Lowest cost is not the same as lowest price."},{"n":75,"u":7,"topic":"Sustaining competitive advantage","ans":"A","stem":["Which one of the following is most likely to help a business sustain a competitive advantage over a long period?"],"table":null,"tpos":1,"opts":["A patent on a unique technology","A price cut that competitors can easily copy","A short-term advertising campaign","A fall in raw material prices that affects all firms in the industry"],"work":null,"right":"A competitive advantage lasts when rivals cannot easily copy it. A patent legally stops competitors copying the technology for a number of years.","wrong":{"B":"If competitors can copy it, the advantage disappears quickly.","C":"Short-term by definition – rivals can respond with their own campaigns.","D":"It helps every firm equally, so it gives no advantage over competitors."},"trap":null},{"n":76,"u":7,"topic":"Achieving cost leadership","ans":"C","stem":["Which one of the following would most help a business to achieve cost leadership?"],"table":null,"tpos":1,"opts":["Using a celebrity to endorse the product","Adding premium features to the product","Gaining purchasing economies of scale by bulk buying","Extending opening hours with extra staff"],"work":null,"right":"Bulk buying lowers the cost of each unit of materials, which helps the business become the lowest-cost producer.","wrong":{"A":"Celebrity endorsement adds cost and supports differentiation.","B":"Premium features add cost and support differentiation.","D":"Extra staff add cost. Longer hours might improve service, which is closer to differentiation."},"trap":null},{"n":77,"u":7,"topic":"Porter and PED","ans":"A","stem":["Statement 1: ‘A successful differentiation strategy should make demand for the product more price inelastic.’","Statement 2: ‘A cost leadership strategy is likely to suit a market where customers are very sensitive to price.’","Read statements 1 and 2 and select the correct option from the following:"],"table":null,"tpos":3,"opts":["Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is false. Statement 2 is false."],"work":null,"right":"Both are true. Differentiation makes the product seem unique, so there are fewer close substitutes and customers are less sensitive to price. Cost leadership suits price-sensitive markets, where being able to charge less (or match rivals profitably) wins customers.","wrong":{"B":"Statement 2 is true.","C":"Statement 1 is true.","D":"Both are true."},"trap":"Being able to link Porter to price elasticity is useful in essays as well as MCQs."},{"n":78,"u":7,"topic":"Ansoff: product development","ans":"C","stem":["A sportswear brand sells trainers in the UK. It launches a range of smartwatches, sold through its existing UK shops to its existing customers.","According to Ansoff’s matrix, this is an example of:"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["market penetration.","market development.","product development.","diversification."],"work":null,"right":"A new product sold to the existing market is product development.","wrong":{"A":"The smartwatch is a new product, so this is not market penetration.","B":"The customers and shops are the same, so the market is not new.","D":"Diversification needs a new market as well as a new product. The customers here are existing ones."},"trap":"Check the product and the market separately before deciding."},{"n":79,"u":7,"topic":"Differentiation: profit per unit","ans":"D","stem":["A business sells a product for £80 with a unit cost of £50. It adopts a differentiation strategy. This lets it raise the price to £100, but the unit cost rises to £62.","What happens to profit per unit?"],"table":null,"tpos":2,"opts":["It rises by £20","It falls by £12","It does not change","It rises by £8"],"work":["Before: £80 − £50 = £30 profit per unit","After: £100 − £62 = £38 profit per unit","Change = £38 − £30 = +£8"],"right":"Differentiation raised the price by more than it raised costs, so profit per unit (value added) increases.","wrong":{"A":"This only looks at the price rise and ignores the higher costs.","B":"This only looks at the cost rise and ignores the higher price.","C":"Price and cost rise by different amounts, so profit per unit changes."},"trap":"Differentiation only works if customers will pay more than the extra it costs."},{"n":80,"u":7,"topic":"Ansoff: market penetration","ans":"D","stem":["Which one of the following is an example of a market penetration strategy?"],"table":null,"tpos":1,"opts":["Selling existing products in a new country","Launching a new product in a new market","Launching a new product for existing customers","Introducing a loyalty card so existing customers buy existing products more often"],"work":null,"right":"Market penetration means increasing sales of existing products in existing markets, for example by getting existing customers to buy more often.","wrong":{"A":"New market, existing product – market development.","B":"New product, new market – diversification.","C":"New product, existing market – product development."},"trap":null}]};
